# Frontend rules

Read `PROJECT-CONSTITUTION.md`, `SOURCE-OF-TRUTH.md`, `OWNERSHIP.md`, `CURRENT-WORK.md`, issue #16 and `docs/ui/` before editing the product UI.

## Preserve the identity spine

- Do not break `/auth/login`, `/auth/callback`, `/auth/logout` or the current server-side session flow.
- Do not expose tokens or session cookies to client components.
- Keep durable AccessLobby person identity distinct from email, phone, username and Keycloak private identifiers.
- Authentication does not grant peer-resource authorization.

## Truthful UI

- Never turn visual-reference values into production facts.
- Use live data only from approved contracts.
- Render explicit signed-out, unavailable, denied, empty, loading and error states.
- Never fall back from a failed live request to synthetic success data.
- Keep development fixtures isolated and disabled by default in production.

## Structure

- Prefer reusable shell, navigation, feedback and primitive components over page duplication.
- Keep feature code grouped by product capability.
- Put server-only data access in server modules/components.
- Use typed adapters between routes/components and backend contracts.
- Avoid adding global dependencies or editing shared auth contracts without explicit review.

## Responsive and accessible

- Build mobile, tablet and desktop behavior intentionally.
- Prevent page-level horizontal overflow.
- Use semantic landmarks and heading order.
- Provide a skip link and visible keyboard focus.
- Give navigation, buttons, form controls and status indicators accessible names.
- Do not rely on color alone.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Maintain useful touch target sizes.

## Scope control

The first production wave is Personal Account Overview, Identity Profile and Recovery & Trusted Contacts. Connected Apps, App Permissions, Launcher, Trust and Insights may influence component architecture but require separate contracts and approval before live activation.

## Required checks

Before declaring UI work complete, run the web typecheck, tests and production build, then capture browser evidence at representative desktop and mobile widths. Re-smoke login, account resolution and logout in staging after deployment.
